From 90edcab36a61ab33bf12ed3a4329774af48990c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeongkyu Shin <jshin@lablup.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:50:29 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] feat(rocm): implement Hadamard for power-of-two sizes up to
 8192

Hadamard was a NO_GPU stub. hadamard.hip adds a single-stage radix-2 butterfly in shared memory for m == 1 and a power-of-two last axis up to 8192, which covers the head dimensions (64, 128, 256) of Hadamard-rotated KV caches. Larger n and the m in {12, 20, 28} families throw a message naming the limit rather than returning a wrong answer; the CUDA backend covers them by generating a radix-m codelet through NVRTC. The scale is the primitive's scale_ (MLX defaults it to 1/sqrt(n)), accumulation is in float for every element type, and like the CUDA kernel the grid is clamped at 65535 blocks with a grid-stride loop.

Checked against the Metal backend on an M1 Ultra: norm ratio 1.000000 within 1e-5 at n = 64, 128 and 256; float16 relative RMS about 2.0e-4 (Metal 3.8e-4 to 5.0e-4, because this kernel keeps the butterfly in float and rounds once); float32 relative RMS about 1e-7.
